 "text": "Your four o'clock coffee is still in you at bedtime. You can probably still fall asleep. That's not the problem. There's one time on the clock that fixes this and most people never said it. Here's the math. Caffeine takes about five hours to drop by half. A cup at four, at nine, half of it is still in you. At two in the morning, a quarter. So you sleep, but caffeine can keep that sleep lighter. You wake up tired and you reach for another coffee. So where's the line? Take your bedtime and count back eight hours. Bed at 11, your cutoff is 3 p.m. After that, it's decaf or nothing. And when the four o'clock slump hits, take a 10-minute walk outside instead. It wakes you up and it doesn't follow you to bed. Swap just that one cup. And at bedtime, you're down from half a cup to about an eighth. Here's the rule. Bedtime minus eight hours. Decaf after that. A walk for the slump. Send this to the friend who orders a latte at five. That's what Demure is for. You archive your dream today so you can see it tomorrow and break it down into small steps you can actually do. Follow for one fix a day for the things that keep stalling. Tomorrow, what to do when it's still dark when you wake up. If you want to try it out, I've put the link in the bio.",
